#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476" :question/problem "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/defb480233b4e48f90cb07fef771f2fdb62b6d7d5cb116393d74518eb3b37db8.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bb62b77da84018c07d4f3dfefc297684974db78b326bd0736469dc83572ae5b6.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478" :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/22ef70dcb0f7d3cba1c9d5514b40800144eb97edd004041ebb3516062a9e3e48.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/d1c2b9c36ec942a1c4113f1ff3a6221028f36c6ac4d109351ebab6bb48f358c4.png) Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/bf65510b8cef0e0162e3054bcf31405ac4e4268a759f6493a06c9f0314c1d482.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2eb3398-ae19-5ca2-ab18-d8935136c5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333546} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0b06b3-08a2-5110-ad50-78ebb74c0b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333547} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9a45ec-a2d4-5173-9908-784eaabd280e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333548} {:answer/id #uuid "a60a30d1-8ebf-5d01-9942-d439f4339c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333549} {:answer/id #uuid "57a94b9a-0482-5936-b6e8-ad04da8947d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2eb3398-ae19-5ca2-ab18-d8935136c5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333546} :answer-field/id #uuid "34016eca-7fa9-5357-b97a-23860f2ff5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b22af89b-1092-506b-925c-dc472e0f7d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106744" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c8a6a6-00b3-5209-9404-fe4e49caa4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333553} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f88fe4-192b-567f-b245-562314735e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333554} {:answer/id #uuid "837a21aa-208c-555d-8278-3c0f18f93a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333555} {:answer/id #uuid "11c0fb27-de6d-5ff5-9bef-38221589ffdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333556} {:answer/id #uuid "1b503461-8a70-5baf-846b-ad7838df13d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c0fb27-de6d-5ff5-9bef-38221589ffdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333556} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6080b57-b10d-5dd9-9f73-e2d70d0903f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea75fe73-8a8e-588a-8eaf-18860230f59a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106828" :question/problem "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k-1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =(k-1)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4k-4 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-16=4k-4, \\\\ -4k+4=-4k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-12=4k\\,⟹\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nThe second equation is true for any value of $k.$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9b63d32-598c-5d60-bd19-6f5ddba8386d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/e4ec74d60e6fdec5dec663ec4fe8f80ad9408817aa34afad916672a5221cdfad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333560} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e78486-bd67-5715-9de7-2e2db1550893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/c59aa1f83fb6808cb6ea5ea2905c120b4b6c2af3a4e3850dc20d7f550f0259eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333561} {:answer/id #uuid "e86d89bb-4f45-513e-b5c1-c38bdfb523f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/a3abd0ff1603a63a670252580ba7edb900b61e669aa12691ca912183c28899ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333562} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cd5905-b5e0-5694-8d78-70a30d65cb63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/aeb84eb023dcc22cc0a7b7713c5a78f76ba5469cd147471c99af6197d7806c30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333563} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe8656e-f1db-5caf-8a21-48ef60b32eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0e38721ff6fe7b75bef46079d0c877333a0113cbc782a12f61e09b832200943a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfe8656e-f1db-5caf-8a21-48ef60b32eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0e38721ff6fe7b75bef46079d0c877333a0113cbc782a12f61e09b832200943a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333564} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fe92464-bfb8-577f-89c8-de5976473ba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f38fa3e-b402-5304-8378-88160f430aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-106886" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/59bb15a433f4f9299a240fd0c4d03797425994a0aa17d69fa81cf92e6f99cabc.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Bu.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Bu & =\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0e38721ff6fe7b75bef46079d0c877333a0113cbc782a12f61e09b832200943a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb6e56ea-0d61-55b9-8f84-eeacb7876fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333567} {:answer/id #uuid "5fea0568-fd79-54b6-b262-69c413e50d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333568} {:answer/id #uuid "fc315d45-49bc-5375-aee1-626dee300b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333569} {:answer/id #uuid "858f42fe-fd05-5e56-bc2a-6b512d0a9592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333570} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfc9749-7bb3-5732-b11e-fc007a2e1961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc315d45-49bc-5375-aee1-626dee300b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333569} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b58fca-a3bd-50c8-b716-40af28eeea21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a739e5b2-a47b-5126-9315-a2ade3bc34d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106946" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1-4c \\\\ -2+8c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1-4c=-3 \\\\ -2+8c=6c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-4c=-3\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2+8c=6c\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc62fd0-3b60-57eb-8863-175b86f15892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I,II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333574} {:answer/id #uuid "050aeaf4-474e-55e2-ab64-880c94331f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333575} {:answer/id #uuid "85645c94-69f7-593d-b964-e7d13a1fa4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333576} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f171a4-6f03-562c-bcaf-6ff6eddbbfc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333577} {:answer/id #uuid "12acfd79-a1f3-5bd4-aa74-2c3e7718990b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85645c94-69f7-593d-b964-e7d13a1fa4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333576} :answer-field/id #uuid "97784497-097e-5492-8fdb-087a789773db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90a94767-f714-5ec0-8fe8-2166e2400350" :question/math-academy-id "q-107137" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=5v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=5{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{5}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c02a682-2a9c-5407-ac35-7dd5f73b0267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333581} {:answer/id #uuid "f99a5d98-82ee-5fb8-84ea-cb3f5f274ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333582} {:answer/id #uuid "0aca831b-f409-5aa2-95e9-02ddde517ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333583} {:answer/id #uuid "84c16fc6-7bd1-57e5-940a-387679a83103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333584} {:answer/id #uuid "b94c8553-8d5e-59f2-9938-a9ffbb4d45d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84c16fc6-7bd1-57e5-940a-387679a83103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333584} :answer-field/id #uuid "df4de464-3831-5932-8664-eec48a32b93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "909b03c3-e39a-5b18-a828-a5284cd424be" :question/math-academy-id "q-107819" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a18a2a3-5973-5132-8c42-b2a21fc869ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333588} {:answer/id #uuid "5406d636-6ca2-505d-a7cf-d3fc9dcae63c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333589} {:answer/id #uuid "4714ba84-bb14-5b18-a5f9-f0fb0ec8743f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333590} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee5723e-a201-50d2-a717-0ecc63e3f9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333591} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5c6f6b-daec-5dda-adb1-f2ce1f8742c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5406d636-6ca2-505d-a7cf-d3fc9dcae63c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333589} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a658887-26f5-54db-bfce-82ec8034d797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "394982ad-ea62-5428-8f14-995c0f4069c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-107837" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e721d1c-2b5d-5774-b4d1-305b4f6f7273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} {:answer/id #uuid "4b788941-acff-5edc-8fbc-6c557d5ffe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333596} {:answer/id #uuid "77f6ae69-73a4-5bcf-a824-96a0026a0c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333597} {:answer/id #uuid "533ea0a8-594b-5a20-af3f-dda0eb6c2e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333598} {:answer/id #uuid "07ce042c-ddf2-57a0-8e76-1832f4c73de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e721d1c-2b5d-5774-b4d1-305b4f6f7273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} :answer-field/id #uuid "965f540b-5be5-5b7a-9514-ee1681c35330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93364803-f932-5599-886a-b33bb951f95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18233" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $D$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Dv=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Dv & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8 & 10 \\\\ -7 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] & =6[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8c+140 \\\\ -7c+154\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8c+140=6c \\\\ -7c+154=84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8c+140=6c\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7c+154=84\\,⟹\\,c=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaca6a4e-aefd-574a-8afb-a476135d2895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333602} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7c324b-ec86-5589-a079-7af025d55328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333603} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfaaa5e-dc74-5742-95e5-436b2715d23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333604} {:answer/id #uuid "1b23df11-6b55-5c47-8402-d911a1800e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333605} {:answer/id #uuid "c5317a8b-5c94-5072-9b2e-8d4bfda5b32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaca6a4e-aefd-574a-8afb-a476135d2895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f255a0b3-0887-5cad-ba83-f26df3e05d72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6af12d90-4cfe-54e1-b057-a8d41286b545" :question/math-academy-id "q-36678" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(2v)=2(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=2(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $-2$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02808189-8f96-53c7-879c-1bedd397bd7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103860} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd2dcb4-2ae3-5ae6-ba53-17fa6c9410c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103861} {:answer/id #uuid "245c7078-4e40-5ed3-90d8-00dc37077685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103862} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9ebb0c-3693-5995-a84f-0eb23c20eedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103863} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf3f92-46cc-50fe-bfe9-749324312ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "245c7078-4e40-5ed3-90d8-00dc37077685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e26d64-6546-5ff0-a419-2696fe342729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4dcf6a4-b235-571e-8987-add1af756339" :question/math-academy-id "q-36791" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=5v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122875ce-dbc1-54af-8cb3-cb09cc02ae34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333609} {:answer/id #uuid "93894d11-c361-5010-b3cb-705ff9b5d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333610} {:answer/id #uuid "c796dfd5-92b9-59fb-8447-cc9d8ff4fded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333611} {:answer/id #uuid "541acfa6-7766-5bd7-af1d-d460e739123f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333612} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7f4ecf-0acb-5f55-a8e8-5ced03a8a965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93894d11-c361-5010-b3cb-705ff9b5d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333610} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c82669f-d84e-5499-9337-132d6c303242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aae12eda-62c6-5db8-83be-eba7232a765e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36865" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $5v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=3v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=3{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{3}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(5v)=5Av=5(-3v)=-3(5v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(5v)=-3(5v),$ and $5v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $5v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca64db74-26d1-581e-9a2c-e81c3427a66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/3c202b0f2c679121f4c51a57514aea8a8d6bf5706e67e4d8986b9ec624a74574.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333616} {:answer/id #uuid "44747833-254d-5f13-a47a-98bd83b202fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/e56f6feb61896097941f2df3ff6b38dff9972820180f571383635d29b95ba8bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333617} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7beb28-e356-52a3-a852-c95cdfa64e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/8e53fd4d2bcde7cbe59d4be00f8e6a14a9f3bc979388791c46e0faef3d51d158.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333618} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3aa9a-7904-5f73-8cb0-7b6d870e601c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/7e74dbc626472e00e42db273ef7d005ec9b3103f8708214871912382fed66663.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333619} {:answer/id #uuid "a856b48d-97ef-598d-9795-28f3fcb1137e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/221b02b2df0cd292a41593f52b96be20c0ed20c1b1ccad080157f4729d871f0c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a856b48d-97ef-598d-9795-28f3fcb1137e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/221b02b2df0cd292a41593f52b96be20c0ed20c1b1ccad080157f4729d871f0c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333620} :answer-field/id #uuid "822f3073-ebee-579a-aa95-00660941f6a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "885a86b4-4c0a-595d-852f-a00f02fd8acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-36869" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/0fb9b5442b5721d4dc273978f30fa79d5b5555faac0672ea44e11ebf79387d6c.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $1.$ Find the vector $Av.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/221b02b2df0cd292a41593f52b96be20c0ed20c1b1ccad080157f4729d871f0c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db08a0b-3982-5901-90c5-936ec024a06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103872} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02100d-0faa-5080-b26f-3a1e1f37effc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103873} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a883fa-f40b-50f2-aed9-590889bc3657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103874} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdfe14e-4427-52cc-b5aa-127f564fdd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103875} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffe6ce5-21ee-5b02-b86e-4dc6df16a4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9db08a0b-3982-5901-90c5-936ec024a06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7227bce-36c9-53c5-9bd0-8756564bb7ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c27f53bd-6979-5472-9b95-f9c6b5cc1260" :question/math-academy-id "q-37087" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ then $3v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-7.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-7v)=-7(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-7(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement III is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a85bca4c-844d-5930-b624-471f31e71e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333623} {:answer/id #uuid "b548fd6f-e362-5cb9-abd4-329ba153de76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333624} {:answer/id #uuid "57731619-6376-5f0a-a424-c82749eeb377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333625} {:answer/id #uuid "11fcfe37-2def-53e2-aed3-15a5bbfecfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333626} {:answer/id #uuid "506576fd-c46f-5f76-8a9e-0551f748436e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a85bca4c-844d-5930-b624-471f31e71e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333623} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fdd86d-1d05-5807-a30a-fb5f7bfa20cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "871a89c7-3af9-5b70-b051-4f7fed74e27c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37090" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $5$ then $10v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $5.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $6.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(10v)=10Av=10(5v)=5(10v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(10v)=5(10v),$ and $10v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $10v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=6v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =6.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5952ab21-3b0c-50b9-913b-305ff7c06b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333630} {:answer/id #uuid "a2697a81-d07b-54e2-808d-a906b013899a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333631} {:answer/id #uuid "0e40e7bc-64dc-58df-8e7b-9aae720090bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333632} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0f7913-f9a3-5e9c-9c60-430574c79c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333633} {:answer/id #uuid "09721ffc-5370-5014-9cba-533e43e4db02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5952ab21-3b0c-50b9-913b-305ff7c06b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333630} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ee20386-319d-5f3f-9497-b8386be5dbc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "740777a5-43a4-5203-8f0b-72f91859f44e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70917" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda  \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b59e3321-188d-5167-b2d0-db7db8656153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333637} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc86d0c-6652-5585-8c8e-801a21c7fcae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333638} {:answer/id #uuid "5a878ef1-ea20-5e0a-a290-00309d277797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333639} {:answer/id #uuid "070a7758-3716-5b99-b438-d604be8b25ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333640} {:answer/id #uuid "a1dc343e-6533-5d79-83a8-e4fb1462f063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a878ef1-ea20-5e0a-a290-00309d277797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333639} :answer-field/id #uuid "70547335-d416-5207-aba4-5d79c2b8d945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700440da-12e4-50fe-86c5-d292870ad78e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75589" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}11=3\\lambda  \\\\ 13=-2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7a572b-76f3-5005-96a4-612f4632f248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333644} {:answer/id #uuid "d8354623-60bd-5c96-b3ee-a96c0914763b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333645} {:answer/id #uuid "509d9686-b782-5098-8d44-67d9307adb25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333646} {:answer/id #uuid "f21a93e6-e6c6-52e5-8094-d37f812e5250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333647} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fa832a-6af9-55f6-8d16-c29a44ab45e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a7a572b-76f3-5005-96a4-612f4632f248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333644} :answer-field/id #uuid "cce53c32-fd8c-59de-8a66-cc917fff76c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7f901fa-248f-5ae5-86ed-bb1e2ea109ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-75610" :question/problem "An eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =5k-4$ is $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(5k-4)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2(3k-1) \\\\ -10+8(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2(5k-4) \\\\ (5k-4)(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6k \\\\ 24k-18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10k-8 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6k=10k-8 \\\\ 24k-18=15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n6k=10k-8\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24k-18 & =15{k}^{2}-17k+4 \\\\ 24\\cdot 2-18 & =15\\cdot {2}^{2}-17\\cdot 2+4 \\\\ 30 & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd2ab1b-e740-5e03-8468-7e6f60e768ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/44ce64acad55425e3d0056653ac06cb3fb37d691a425b1ef32e69b29a47074be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333651} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fcf25e-73ce-5c90-86b4-286522b41b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/8aa05de3863b1b4f840ee45b2ddc91ea2273de645a5add2fe127b1499e4a641f.png" :